  "step_2": "The individual describes the decision as close ('Knappe Entscheidung') and comments that the 100 Euro difference (between 2500 and 2400) is not very significant given that it occurs with a 2/3 (approximately 66%) probability. The individual appears to focus on the 2400 Euro outcome in B (which occurs with 66%) and compares it to the certain 2400 in A, noting that the higher payoff of 2500 in B is not compelling due to its moderate probability (33%, not 2/3 — possibly a misperception or simplification). The justification centers on the similarity of outcomes and the moderate chance of gaining an extra 100 Euros, implying that the certainty of 2400 Euros may have been appealing. No mention is made of the 1% chance of 0 Euros or the rare outcomes. The deliberation focuses on the central outcomes and their probabilities, particularly the 66% and 33% chances, and downplays the value of the 100 Euro gain due to its likelihood.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires attention to the least probable outcome in each lottery — specifically, the outcome with the smallest probability. In Lottery B, this is 0 Euros with 1%; in Lottery A, all outcomes are certain, so the least probable outcome is still 2400 Euros with 100%. The reason would favor A because its least probable outcome (2400) is better than B's (0). However, in the verbal report, the individual does not mention the 0 Euro outcome or the 1% risk at all. Instead, the focus is on the 66% and 33% outcomes and the marginal value of the extra 100 Euros. The individual's justification revolves around the central, more likely outcomes, not the rarest ones. There is no indication that the least probable outcome was considered, which is central to the decision reason. Therefore, the information relevant to the reason was not considered, and the justification does not align with the rule of the reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

{
